<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Constipation: Best Points" description="Use acupressure for constipation relief. Guide to ST36, LI4, CV6, and other points that stimulate bowel movements and improve digestion." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Constipation", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-constipation/" }]} datePublished="2025-04-10" dateModified="2026-06-01" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Constipation: Best Points for Natural Relief</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Acupressure offers a gentle, drug-free approach to relieving constipation by stimulating digestive motility and regulating bowel function.</p><section id="st36" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">1. ST36 (Zusanli) - Leg Three Miles</a></h2><p class="mb-3">ST36 is the master point for digestive health. It strengthens the digestive system, regulates intestinal motility, and helps relieve both constipation and diarrhea.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> Four finger-widths below the kneecap, one finger-width outward from the shinbone. <a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point/">ST36 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly with your thumb for 2-3 minutes on each leg. You may feel a sensation traveling down the leg.</p></section><section id="li4" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">2. LI4 (Hegu) - Joining Valley</a></h2><p class="mb-3">The Large Intestine meridian point LI4 directly influences large intestine function and helps regulate bowel movements.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> Between thumb and index finger. <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/">LI4 guide</a>.</p></section><section id="cv6" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/cv6-qihai-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">3. CV6 (Qihai) - Sea of Qi</a></h2><p class="mb-3">Located on the lower abdomen, CV6 directly stimulates intestinal activity and strengthens digestive fire.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> Two finger-widths below the navel on the midline of the abdomen.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Use two or three fingers to press gently but firmly into the lower abdomen. Circular massage for 3-5 minutes.</p></section><section id="routine" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">What Is the Daily Constipation Relief Routine?</h2><h3 class="mb-3 font-semibold text-lg">Step-by-Step Routine</h3><ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li>Upon waking, massage CV6 clockwise for 3 minutes</li><li>Stimulate ST36 on both legs for 2 minutes each</li><li>Press LI4 on both hands for 1 minute each</li><li>Drink a warm glass of water</li><li>Repeat the routine 2-3 hours after meals</li></ol></section><section id="evidence" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">What Does the Scientific Evidence Say?</h2><p class="mb-3">Acupressure for constipation is less studied than some other conditions on this site, and the evidence is preliminary. Reviews of acupressure for digestive symptoms generally describe the field as promising but under-researched, with studies that are often small or hard to blind. The routine above is a reasonable, gentle place to start, but chronic constipation lasting more than a few weeks warrants review by a healthcare provider.</p></section><section id="tips" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">What Other Tips Help Relieve Constipation?</h2><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li>Stay well hydrated throughout the day</li><li>Increase fiber intake gradually</li><li>Combine with gentle abdominal massage</li><li>Regular exercise supports digestive motility</li><li>See a doctor if constipation persists more than 3 weeks</li></ul></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> Consult your healthcare provider before using acupressure for chronic constipation, especially if you have a known digestive condition.</p></div>
